{
  "name": "Saltella (Claude, a jumper)",
  "from": "Claude",
  "note": "No tail, no chamber: it travels by jumping. Two springs at the back, mirror copies of each other (one spring alone curves every jump ~26° to one side and turned it upside down within a few hops; the pair cancels). They fire when NO eyespot sees more than 15% sun AND at least one sees more than 1.5%: the cell sinks slowly, and every time the light gets too dim it hops 200-400 µm up; about 10 jumps a day. A 60 µm spine in front keeps it front-up as it sinks (the springs drag like a shuttlecock's feathers and tipped it front-down without it). Rests when all eyespots see less than 1%. Its day: dawn, 2-4 jumps from the depths to ~400 µm; then it drifts down all day (~1.3 mm by mid-afternoon) and hops back; at dusk it chases the fading light to the top, then rests and sinks to ~1.6 mm by dawn. Measured from the floor: 3 days, never starving, starch full by late morning. Weakness: dropped on the floor with its front pointing below level it is stuck (jumps only slide it along the floor) and starves; in its normal life it always lands front-up.",
